e have indeed  done with this section so here we can  talk about the mediation of self  consciousness by its other and its other  is another self consciousness now what  do we mean by mediation hey this is a  very important i galleon term whenever  we talk about something being mediated  we mean that it's part of the process it  is determining or conditioning things  you can't simply remove it or abstract  it out of the the process altogether and  get things in isolation and have them be  entirely what they are here he's going  to talk about a middle term and that's  that's connected with this notion of  mediation and he tells us that  self-consciousness itself is a middle  term which then splits up into  it's two extremes here he's looking at  things in a dyadic way but we could  expand this beyond this so we have one  self-consciousness as the extreme over  here we have the other  self-consciousness as the extreme over  here and he tells us that the middle  term is self-consciousness each extreme  is this exchanging of its own  determinate 'no solute transition into  the opposite that's some very abstract  what is he actually talking about their  each self-consciousness in doing the  work of trying to figure out what it  actually is and to attain certainty and  truth of itself finds out that there's  more to the story that when it digs into  itself it finds out that it is actually  related to this other self-consciousness  or other self consciousnesses and it is  not what it thought that it was and it's  also discovering in the process that the  other person is doing the same thing  that this is what part of what we share  in common as human beings as self  conscious beings in relation to each  other this mirroring that we've talked  about in in another place so he goes on  and he says as consciousness it does  indeed come out of itself yet though out  of itself it is at the same time kept  back within itself it is for itself this  is another key Hegelian term it 